        }
      ],
      "correctOrder": [
        "t3",
        "t1",
        "t4",
        "t2"
      ],
      "explanation": "'Mujhe samajh nahin aaya' — 'mujhe' (to me), 'samajh' (understanding), 'nahin' (not), 'aaya' (came). Literally: 'To me understanding did not come'."
    },
    {
      "id": "hi-s1-l3-ex07",
      "type": "multiple_choice",
      "question": "What is the difference between 'shukriya' and 'dhanyavaad'?",
      "options": [
        "'Dhanyavaad' is more formal than 'shukriya'",
        "'Shukriya' is more formal than 'dhanyavaad'",
        "They mean completely different things",
        "'Dhanyavaad' means sorry"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "explanation": "Both mean 'thank you', but 'dhanyavaad' is more formal and comes from Sanskrit, while 'shukriya' comes from Urdu and is used in everyday conversation."
    },
